From febae7d0552eeb29d777e01034b3ade91bc2501c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 09:38:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH v0 09/10] Multiply short numerics without going through
 mul_var()

Products of stored columns -- price * quantity and the like -- multiply
operands of a few NBASE digits.  For those, numeric_mul() spends far more on
its machinery than on the arithmetic: it unpacks both operands into
NumericVars, has mul_var() allocate a digit buffer for the product, strips
it, copies it into a second palloc'd chunk in make_result() and frees the
first.  Add numeric_mul_short(), which reads both operands where they lie,
multiplies the digits in a local array and builds the result with a single
palloc.  It handles short-format operands of up to five digits; anything
else -- special values, long headers, wider operands, compressed or
out-of-line datums -- takes the existing path.

Five digits is where the simple loop stops paying off: for wider operands
the unrolled code of mul_var_short() is as fast or faster.  Both operands
are checked before either is decoded, so that a rejected product -- a short
price times a wide amount, say -- does not first pay for unpacking the
operand that did qualify.

The result is the general path's to the bit: the product of two such
operands is exact in at most ten digits, its display scale (the sum of the
operand scales) is at most 126 and so is never rounded, and the header is
chosen by the same NUMERIC_CAN_BE_SHORT() test make_result() applies.  The
new regression test shows value, scale and stored size for the edge cases,
including the two that need a long header, and compares ten thousand random
products of zero to eight digit operands against numeric_mul_safe().

The fast path also sidesteps a performance trap that an earlier commit,
"Unpack short numerics on the stack in arithmetic and aggregation", ran
into.  The header tests in numeric_mul_safe() and init_var_from_num() --
"n_header & NUMERIC_SIGN_MASK" on a uint16 -- compile under gcc to 16-bit
instructions with a 16-bit immediate, which stall the legacy x86 decoder
(a length-changing prefix) whenever that code is not served from the uop
cache.  Unpacking on the stack changed nothing in that code, but shifted it,
and it moved out of the uop cache.  On Emerald Rapids, per row of
count(qty * price), decode.lcp went from 0.01 to 11.9 and legacy-decoded uops
from 23 to 88; that ate the whole gain from the saved allocations, and on
another machine turned it into a loss.  Vanilla built with
-falign-functions=64 stalls too, 4.9 times per row, so the hazard is layout
luck rather than anything specific to that commit.
numeric_mul_short() tests its flag bits with a shift, which gcc emits without
the prefix, and a product it accepts no longer reaches numeric_mul_safe() at
all.  A product it rejects still does, and still stalls there.
